course: dictionary
lesson: 4557
title: Heavy: weight, amount and feeling
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Heavy has several meanings, and the words around it tell you which one you need. Its first meaning is about weight: a heavy thing weighs a lot and is hard to lift. Heavier means weighs more, and heaviest means weighs the most of all.
explain: Read this: «A moving swing is heavy and fast at a child's head height and can knock you out or break teeth, so walk wide around swings.» The clues are fast, knock you out and break teeth. Here heavy means a lot of weight moving with force, so it is dangerous.
explain: Heavy can also mean a lot of or strong, as in «heavy rain» or «heavy traffic». Try swapping in "a lot of": a lot of rain still makes sense. The clue is the word after heavy, which tells you what there is a lot of.
explain: Heavy can mean sad or serious too. In «a heavy heart», nothing is really weighing anything down. The clue is the feeling: a heart that feels heavy is carrying a sad, serious feeling.
fact: Heavy means weighing a lot and hard to lift; heavier means weighs more, and heaviest means weighs the most of all.
fact: Heavy also means a lot of or strong, as in «heavy rain» and «heavy traffic».
fact: A «heavy meal» is one that makes you feel full, and a big, heavy meal late at night can disturb sleep.
fact: Heavy can mean sad or serious, as in «a heavy heart».
fact: Heavy things are not always big: a small stone can be heavier than a big balloon.
exercise: Which is heavier: a small stone or a big balloon? | They weigh exactly the same | The big balloon, because it is bigger | *The small stone
exercise: French says heavy rain is «il pleut des cordes», "it's raining ropes". In «heavy rain», what does heavy mean? | *A lot of, or strong | Weighing a lot, like a heavy box | Full after eating | Sad and serious
exercise: «A secret can grow heavier over time because it has nowhere to go.» What does heavier suggest? | Smaller and lighter | *Harder to carry, a bigger worry | Easier to lift and carry | Louder
test: A pebble is heavy, a brick is heavier, and a rock is the ___ of the three. Which word fits the gap? | heavy | *heaviest | heavily | heavier
test: In «It rained heavily all afternoon», what kind of word is heavily? | *An adverb, describing how it rained | A colour | A name for a thing | A question
exam: Which sentence uses heavy to mean sad or serious? | Eat a heavy meal slowly | *She carried a heavy heart after the news | The heavy rain stopped after lunch | Lift the heavy box carefully
